// case: generic-model-type-parameter
// what: `model Box[T] { v: T }` used to pass `orbit check` with no errors and
//      then fail in the C step on `unknown type name 'T'`. Nothing
//      monomorphises, so `T` reached the emitter as an ordinary type name and
//      `mapTypeToC` had no case for it: a bare `T*` in the struct, in the
//      constructor and in every register holding a field read, while the
//      surrounding register declarations helpfully cast it to `void*`.
//
//        $ orbit build gen.orb -o gen
//          <build>:31:5: error: unknown type name 'T'
//             31 |     T* v;
//
//      The compiler is now the one that says no, and it says so about the
//      declaration rather than about generated C. This is the same missing
//      feature as the quarantined std/quarantine/option.orb.quarantined:
//      `Option<T>` cannot be written until codegen substitutes the argument at
//      each use.
//
//      The declaration is where the refusal belongs, not the use site. The
//      positional constructor spelling already refused it -- `Box(1)` is
//      E1003, "Field 'Box.v' is declared T but the argument is int" -- because
//      checkNode happens to compare the field's declared type against the
//      argument's. That is an accident of that spelling: `fn id[T](x: T)` is
//      E1005 for the same reason and `model Box[T] { v: int }` was accepted,
//      because there is no rule anywhere that says a type parameter cannot be
//      a field's declared type. A use-site check that fires only when the use
//      happens to line up is not a check.
//
//      WRITING THE INTENT, which is what makes the feature real: substitute
//      the argument at each use, so `Option<int>` and `Option<string>` lower
//      to distinct C. Then the parser takes the parameter list back and
//      std/quarantine/option.orb.quarantined can be lifted.
// expect-error: 'model Box[T]' is not supported: there is no monomorphisation
model Box[T] {
    v: T
}

fn main() -> int {
    val b = Box(1)
    print(b.v)
    return 0
}
